A||||1||fierospeeder||incometax?  hope, college||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||04-09-2001||10:49 PM||||I was wondering if anyone who is in college filed taxes using that "hope" credit, <BR>its suppose to give you a refund up to 2000 dollars a year. <P>I read a tax book, and im confused on who can file it.  I pay for school plus the books, so can i file for it, eventhough i live with my parents.   My dad did claim me as a dependent, but only used my sister for school credit, 24 bucks.  <P>Question, can i file it myself, i got about 500 bucks spent for school, and also do i have to use the regular form, and not the ez form, i seen the hope credit form already.<BR>Is it even worth to file?<P>Plus can i get a refund on speeding tickets?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Coop88||04-10-2001||12:03 AM||||To claim the credits, you have to use a 1040 or 1040A AND a form 8863. You also have to claim yourself as a dependent. So your dad could claim the hope or lifetime learning credit. The hope credit is good up to $1500, and is only good for the 1st 2 years of college. The lifetime learning credit is used after that, and it's good up to $1000. Both credits depend on your income too. I claim myself as a dependent, and I make about $7000 per year. I got a credit for $32 in 1999, and I think about $25 this year. Basically, I get back all of the federal tax I pay. No, I don't think you can get a refund on speeding tickets. Go to <A HREF="http://www.irs.gov" TARGET=_blank>www.irs.gov</A>  and search for form 8863. It has all the instructions.<P>Coop<p>[This message has been edited by Coop88 (edited 04-10-2001).]||||reg||10
